# 🛡️ Project 3: Governance & Security Guardrails

## 🗺️ Lab Objective
Establish automated control plane guardrails within an enterprise subscription framework. This project engineers a custom Azure Policy definition applied at the resource group scope to automatically deny non-authorized virtual machine sizes, alongside a custom least-privilege RBAC role to restrict operator identities to virtual machine reboot activities.

---

## 🛠️ Step-by-Step Command-Line Execution Blueprint

```bash
# 1. Save the custom blueprint policy parameters file
cat << 'EOF' > allowed-skus.json
{
  "if": {
    "allOf": [
      {
        "field": "type",
        "equals": "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines"
      },
      {
        "field": "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/sku.name",
        "notIn": [
          "Standard_B1s",
          "Standard_B2ats_v2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "effect": "deny"
  }
}
EOF

# 2. Register the Core Azure Policy definition rule container
az policy definition create \
  --name "restrict-vm-skus" \
  --display-name "Restrict VM SKUs to B-Series" \
  --rules allowed-skus.json

# 3. Create the targeted sandbox container room
az group create --name "Marathahalli_Lab_RG" --location "southeastasia"

# 4. Assign the policy enforcer live to the Resource Group scope
az policy assignment create \
  --name "Enforce_B_Series_Only" \
  --policy "restrict-vm-skus" \
  --resource-group "Marathahalli_Lab_RG"

# 5. Build and register the Custom Least-Privilege Identity Role
SUB_ID=$(az account show --query id --output tsv)
cat << EOF > vm-operator-role.json
{
  "Name": "VM Restart Operator",
  "IsCustom": true,
  "Description": "Can only restart VMs.",
  "Actions": [
    "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/read",
    "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/restart/action"
  ],
  "NotActions": [],
  "AssignableScopes": [
    "/subscriptions/$SUB_ID"
  ]
}
EOF

az role definition create --role-definition vm-operator-role.json
```

---

## 🚙 Code Logic Decoder (In Plain, Simple Words)

* **`"field": ".../sku.name", "notIn": [ "Standard_B1s", "Standard_B2ats_v2" ]`**
  This creates an approved whitelist rule. It evaluates incoming deployment requests; if the requested hardware type matches either of these low-cost configurations, it passes the checkpoint. If it reads anything else, it gets caught.
  
* **`"effect": "deny"`**
  This acts as an active gate blocker. Instead of letting a resource deploy and flags it later in a history log, it drops the command instantly, saving your corporate wallet.

* **`"Actions": [ ".../virtualMachines/restart/action" ]`**
  This restricts user access to execution actions. Identities holding this custom role can trigger an infrastructure reboot script, but they cannot write configurations, change passwords, or delete machines.

